Chapter 7 — Part 1
The rest of the matches blurred into noise behind him. Cheers when someone landed a clean capture. Groans when a plan fell apart. All Might’s voice booming encouragement over the speakers. Curtis stayed for the first two, then slipped out while Mina was still replaying Uraraka’s MVP clip on her phone and Bakugo was busy glaring holes through the floor.
The roof access stair was unlocked. He climbed it without rushing. Wind moved across the top of U.A., carrying the distant sounds of Ground Beta up with it. He set the deer-skull mask on the low concrete ledge, antlers casting thin shadows, and pulled a crumpled pack from the inner pocket of the costume. The lighter caught on the second try. He lit the cigarette, took a slow drag, and let the smoke drift.
For a minute he just stood there. Uniform jacket half-unzipped under the armor he’d loosened. Braids shifting in the breeze. The low, steady pull in his chest was still present, quiet as ever. Below, another explosion echoed—someone’s quirk, someone’s win. He didn’t look.
The cigarette burned. He watched the ash gather and flicked it once over the edge. The city stretched past the campus walls. Somewhere out there was a net café booth with his name on an overnight rate, a club that might text him hours, and a principal who already knew more than he said. Up here none of it reached him yet.
He took another drag and stayed where he was.
The door to the roof creaked open behind him.
Curtis didn’t turn right away. He took another drag, smoke curling past the antlers of the mask still resting on the ledge. Footsteps crossed the concrete—light, hesitant—then stopped a few paces back.
“Curtis?”
Uraraka’s voice. He glanced over his shoulder. She stood just clear of the doorway in her hero costume, cheeks already pink from the climb or from finding him, one hand still on the handle like she might leave if he told her to.
“Everyone’s looking for you,” she said. “All Might’s about to wrap up. Aizawa too. Mina thinks you went to the locker room.” Her eyes flicked to the cigarette, then back to his face. “You okay?”
Curtis exhaled toward the open air. “Needed a minute. Matches were loud.” He tapped ash over the edge. “You can tell them I’ll be down.”
She didn’t leave. The wind moved her hair. The low, steady pull that always sat in his chest didn’t change, but she shifted her weight like the quiet up here felt heavier than the monitoring room.
“That punch earlier,” she started, then stopped. Tried again. “You made it look easy. Bakugo didn’t even touch you.” A small smile. “And thanks. For the MVP thing. And the plan.”
“You reached the bomb. That’s the part that counted.” He finally turned enough to look at her properly, cigarette held loose. “I’ll finish this and head back. Don’t let Iida mark me absent.”
Uraraka nodded, still pink. “I’ll save you a seat.” She hesitated one second longer, then slipped back through the door. It clicked shut. The roof went quiet again except for the wind and the distant sound of All Might declaring another winner somewhere below.
Curtis took one last drag, crushed the cigarette under his boot, and picked up the mask. He stayed another moment with the city in front of him, then started for the stairs.
The final bell had already rung. Most of the class had cleared the gates, still riding the high of the battle trials. Curtis walked with his duffel over one shoulder, costume packed away, civilian clothes back on, when the voice caught him.
“Young Beardy!”
All Might stood just off the main path in full muscle form, cape settled, smile present but quieter than the one he’d worn all afternoon. He waited until Curtis stopped.
“A moment, if you have it.”
Curtis shifted the strap. “Sure.”
All Might glanced toward the empty training fields, then back. “You handled the match cleanly. Uraraka earned the MVP, but the opening you made was exact. Bakugo will be angry for a while. That’s his nature.” The smile tilted. “You also left before the final review. Aizawa noticed. So did I.”
“Needed air,” Curtis said. Accent even. “Wasn’t ditching the lesson.”
“I know.” All Might’s voice dropped a notch, the way it had in the clearing. “Cathleen asked me to keep an eye on how you settled. Not as a handler. As someone who’s seen what happens when power like yours doesn’t have a place to land.” He paused. “The offer from before still stands. If you want another measurement—or if Mirko ever answers that request—you know where to find me. And if the roof becomes a habit, at least tell someone you’re up there. U.A. prefers its students breathing.”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Almost a smile. “I’ll keep it in mind.”
All Might nodded, the big heroic posture easing back into place. “Good. Get some rest, young man. Tomorrow’s still a school day.”
He turned toward the campus interior. Curtis watched him go for a second, then adjusted the duffel and kept walking for the gates, the low steady pull in his chest as quiet as it had been all day.
Curtis stopped before All Might had gone more than a few steps.
“Hey, All Might. Does Aizawa not trust me?”
All Might turned. The smile stayed, but it shifted into something more careful. He walked back the short distance so they weren’t raising their voices across the path.
“Aizawa trusts data,” he said. “He trusts what he can measure and what doesn’t put the other students at risk. You gave him a very large measurement today and still haven’t shown him the edges of it. That makes him cautious. Not hostile.” All Might’s eyes were steady. “He argued with Nezu about your placement because he doesn’t like unknowns in his class. Once he sees you’ll follow the limits he sets—and that you won’t freeze or overdo it when it matters—he’ll ease. That’s how he works.”
Curtis absorbed it, expression even. “So it’s not personal.”
“It’s very personal to him,” All Might said, a touch of dry humor. “Just not in the way you’re asking. He lost people by not being careful enough. He won’t do it again with this class. You’re part of that now.” He tilted his head. “Give him the version of you that trains with them instead of past them. The rest will follow.”
Curtis nodded once. “Alright. Thanks.”
“Anytime, young Beardy.” All Might’s smile widened back into the familiar one. “Now go on. Before Aizawa decides the roof counts as an unexcused absence.”
Curtis huffed a short breath and turned toward the gates. All Might watched him long enough to see him clear the path, then headed the other way, cape stirring in the late light.
All Might’s cape disappeared around the corner of the main building. Curtis stood on the path a second longer, duffel strap creaking under his grip.
“How the fuck am I supposed to do that?” The words came out low, rough. “I never had much regular folk to talk to except mutations and cryptids.” He laughed once, short and dry. “Haha. Whatever.”
His phone buzzed. Janice’s name lit the screen—shift reminder. He swore under his breath.
“Ah crap, I’m going to be late for work.”
He took off toward the gates at a run, boots hitting the pavement hard, braids swinging. The campus lights were already coming on. He didn’t look back.
In the shadow of the equipment shed just off the path, someone stood perfectly still. They’d watched the whole exchange—All Might’s careful words, Curtis’s muttered frustration, the sudden sprint. A faint blush sat high on their cheeks. They didn’t step out. They just stayed where they were, eyes following him until he cleared the gates and vanished toward the station, the low passive pull of Free Love making the simple act of watching feel heavier than it should have.
Then the figure slipped deeper into the dark and was gone.
Mina dropped out of the shadow of the shed like she’d been waiting for the perfect entrance, one arm slinging easily over Uraraka’s shoulders.
“Whatcha doing, Ochaco-chan? Spying on the new guy~? Hmm?”
Uraraka yelped, face going scarlet so fast it reached her ears. “M-Mina! I wasn’t—I was just—!”
“Just watching him run off after talking to All Might, all sneaky in the dark?” Mina grinned, sharp and delighted, giving her a little squeeze. “You’ve been pink since the battle trial. And don’t think I missed you sneaking up to the roof earlier. Spill. Is it the height? The voice? The whole ‘I punch Bakugo once and leave’ thing?”
Uraraka covered her face with both hands, muffling a groan. “It’s not— I don’t know! He was just standing there and I… I wanted to see if he was okay. That’s all.”
Mina laughed, bright and unrepentant. “Uh-huh. Sure. Come on, spy girl. Train’s gonna leave without us, and you’re telling me everything on the way. Every. Single. Detail.”
She started steering Uraraka toward the gates, still grinning. Uraraka let herself be pulled, cheeks burning, the low warmth of Free Love still buzzing under her skin while Mina kept up the teasing all the way to the station.
Uraraka peeked through her fingers as they walked, voice small. “Umm, Mina. I sort of noticed the other girls look at him the same way. Do you—”
“Yup.” Mina didn’t even let her finish. She grinned, shameless, and bumped their shoulders together. “And we all think you’re lucky you got to team with him.” She winked. “Next time I’m teaming with him. Fair warning.”
Uraraka’s face went hotter. “Mina!”
“What? Tall, quiet, punches Bakugo into next week, then says ‘well deserved’ like it’s nothing?” Mina laughed. “Half the class is pretending they’re not staring. You’re just the one who got the first match. Don’t worry, I’ll share. Maybe.” She squeezed Uraraka’s shoulder again, still grinning. “Now come on. You’re buying me a taiyaki for emotional damages while you explain exactly how pink you got when he smiled at you.”
Uraraka groaned into her hands but kept walking, the station lights getting closer while Mina’s teasing carried them both down the path.
